[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is architectural, not a bounded change — "notifications system" names a new subsystem, and the repo has no
  existing flow to modify. Full path: questions, approaches, sectioned design, written spec, then writing-plans. No code
  until you approve a design.

  What I found exploring: the repo is a single commit containing one file, [38;5;153mindex.html:1[39m — a static page with an empty
  [38;5;153m<main>[39m. No JavaScript, no task model, no server, no users, no build tooling, no tests.

  That matters for your request. "Users get notified when tasks they care about change" presumes several things that
  don't exist here yet: more than one user, a notion of a task that can change, something that records those changes,
  and somewhere a notification can be delivered. A notifications system is downstream of all of that.

  So before I ask about notification design, I need to know what the surrounding app actually is.

